titre$The genus Hebeloma FUNGI Of NORTHERN EUROPE - VOL. 3
com$The genus Hebeloma, Jan Vesterholt 2005 /adapt GF 2010

Keys to the species KEY TO SECTIONS AND SUBSECTIONS
1,cs10$détails sections
1,0$schémas spores et cystides$$f"hebeloma_Vesterholt.gif"
1,2$Cortina present but often fugacious : caulocystidia only present at the top of the stem : cheilocystidia with a ±cylindrical apical part, often widened in lower half (fig. 5A-B) : perispore never loosening (PO)
1,3$Cortina absent : caulocystidia often continuing to the base of the stem : cheilocystidia often with a different shape : perispore loosening or not (PO-4)
2,cs1$Spores ellipsoid to ellipsoid-ovoid, mostly yellowish in water and KOH mounts, indextrinoid or weakly dextrinoid (DO-l).$Key A - sect. Hebeloma, subsect. Hebeloma, p. 28$s"sect. Hebeloma, subsect. Hebeloma"
2,cs1,n$Cortina present; cheilocystidia with a ±cylindrical apical part, often widened in lower third; spores ellipsoid to ellipsoid-ovoid, indextrinoid or weakly dextrinoid (sect. Hebeloma, subsect. Hebeloma).

2,cs2$Spores ovoid-amygdaliform to citriform, mostly brownish, weakly to strongly dextrinoid (D2-5), rarely almost indextrinoid (Dl).$Key B - sect. Hebeloma, subsect. Amygdalina, p. 29$s"sect. Hebeloma, subsect. Amygdalina"
2,cs2,n$Cortina present; cheilocystidia with a ±cylindrical apical part, often widened in lower third; spores ovoid-amygdaliform to citriform, weakly to strongly dextrinoid (sect. Hebeloma, subsect. Amygdalina).
3,0$Universal veil forming a membranous ring on the stem : stem continuing into a strongly rooting base : smell of marzipan, rarely weak.$H. radicosum, (sect. Myxocybe, subsect. Myxocybe), p. 104
3,4$Universal veil present or absent but never forming a membranous ring on the stem : stem rooting or not : smell not of marzipan
4,cs6$Smell strong and sweet.$Key F - sect. Myxocybe, subsect. Sacchariolentia, p. 32$s"sect. Myxocybe, subsect. Sacchariolentia"
4,cs6,n$Cortina absent : smell strong and sweetish, penetrating (sect. Myxocybe, subsect. Sacchariolentia).


4,5$Smell different (radish-like, cocoa-like, soap-like, tea-like, fruit-like) or indistinct
5,0$Gills with a distinct pinkish tinge, spore deposit brownish red.$H. sarcophyllum, (sect. Porphyrospora), p. 132
5,6$Gills without a pinkish tinge, spore deposit umber, sepia, dark brick or whitish
6,cs5$Fruitbodies often ±rooting : smell never with radish-like components : gills without droplets : cystidia ±cylindrical or variable in shape.$Key E - sect. Myxocybe, subsect, Scabrispora, p. 32$s"sect. Myxocybe, subsect, Scabrispora"
6,cs5,n$Cortina absent : fruitbodies +/- rooting : smell never with radish-like components : cystidia versiform (sect. Myxocybe, subsect. Scabrispora).

6,7$Fruitbodies never rooting : smell usually with radish-like components : gills with or without droplets : cystidia weil differentiated and cylindrical, clavate, capitate or ventricose
7,0$Spore deposit whitish.$H. domardianum (sect. Denudata, subsect. Hebelomina), p. 102
7,8$Spore deposit umber to sepia or dark brick
8,9$Cheilocystidia in majority ventricose with a cylindrical, apical part (fig. SC)
8,10$Cheilocystidia cylindrical to clavate or capitate, sometimes both widened at apex and at base (figs SD-H)
9,0$Stem coarse1y floccose in transverse be1ts, especially in the upper part, not discolouring from the base : nearly ail cheilocystidia ventricose.$H. sinapizans (sect. Sinapizantiai, p. 60
9,0$Stem finely pruinose, especially in the upper part, discolouring from the base : ventricose cheilocystidia are intermixed with clavate cheilocystidia.$H. quercetorum (sect. Yelutipes, subsect. Aestivale), p. 72
10,cs4$Spores indextrinoid or weakly dextrinoid (DO-2).$Key D - sect. Denudata, subsect. Denudata, p. 30 $s"sect. Denudata, subsect. Denudata"
10,cs4,n$Cortina absent : fruitbodies not rooting : sme1l radish-like : cystidia clavate to capitate, often in addition widened in the basal part : spore deposit umber to sepia (sect, Denudata, subsect. Denudata).

10,11$Spores distinctly to strongly dextrinoid (D3-4)
11,0$Spores on average 4.4-5.3 µm broad.$H. theobrominum (sect, Theobromina), p. 62
11,12$Spores on average> 5.5 µm broad
12,cs4$Cheilocystidia typically widened both at apex and base (fig. S H).$Key D - sect. Denudata, subsect. Denudata, p. 30 $s"sect. Denudata, subsect. Denudata"
12,cs4,n$Cortina absent : fruitbodies not rooting : sme1l radish-like : cystidia clavate to capitate, often in addition widened in the basal part : spore deposit umber to sepia (sect, Denudata, subsect. Denudata).

12,13$Cheilocysitida cylindrical to subclavate, typically not much widened at apex or at base (fig. 5 E)
13,cs3$Perispore never or only slightly loosening (PO-1) : stem with a rounded basal bulb.$Key C - sect. Velutipes, subsect. Velutipes, p. 30$s"sect. Velutipes, subsect. Velutipes"
13,cs3,n$Cortina absent : fruitbodies not rooting : smell radish-like : cystidia clavate to capitate : cap dominantly pale, not hygrophanous : spores strongly dextrinoid (D3-4), verruculose (01-2), with a mostly non-loosening perispore (PO-l) (sect, Velutipes, subsect. Velutipes).
13,0$Perispore distinctly loosening in most spores (P3) : stem usually without a basal bulb.$H. aestivale (sect. Velutipes, subsect. Aestivale), p. 68



cs1,0$KEY A: Cortina present : cheilocystidia with a ±cylindrical apical part, often widened in lower third : spores ellipsoid to ellipsoid-ovoid, indextrinoid or weakly dextrinoid (sect. Hebeloma, subsect. Hebeloma).

1,2$In the arctic or alpine zone, usually with Salix species
1,5$In other types of habitat (not arctic or alpine zone)
2,0$Cap umber to sepia, almost to the margin : exsiccata coriaceous.$See H. polare, p. 48
2,3$Cap not so dark, bur often with a dark centre : exsiccata fragile to fairly tough
3,0$Spores on average longer than 10.3 µm and broader than 5.8 µm : cap 10-24 µm broad, not fully expanding.$H. marginatulum, p. 42
3,4$Spores on average shorter than 10.5 µm and narrower than 6.0 µm : cap 15-50 (-70) mm broad, fully expanded with age
4,0$Epicutis mostly about 30 µm thick : stem rather short, only slightly discolouring with age : cap nearly unicoloured clay-buff to Isabella or cinnamon.$H. bruchetii, p. 40
4,0$Epicutis > 50 J.1m thick : stem usually rather long, distinctly discolouring to brown from the base with age : cap typically bicoloured with a cinnamon to yellowish brown or umber centre and a pa 1er margin.$H. mesophaeum, p. 34
5,0$Cap initially fairly pale, ±unicoloured : stem (4-)6-14 mm broad, often becoming sordid with age : not in sand dunes.$H. sordidum, p. 38
5,6$Cap mostly with a clay-buff to cinnamon or umber centre and a paler, often greyish margin : stem often narrower : in sand dunes or in other habitats
6,0$Spores on average < 10.5 µm long.$H. mesophaeum, p. 34
6,7$Spores on average> 10.5 µm long
7,0$Stem pale from the beginning, but not whitish, soon discolouring to brown from the base : sometimes subfasciculate : under Salix, Betula or Populus, often with Salix in coastal dunes.$H. collariatum, p. 44
7,0$Stem white, only weakly discolouring with age : not fasciculate : only recorded with Salix in coastal dunes.$H. psammophilum, p. 46

cs2,0$
rem$KEY B: Cortina present : cheilocystidia with a ±cylindrical apical part, often widened in lower third : spores ovoid-amygdaliform to citriform, weakly to strongly dextrinoid (sect. Hebeloma, subsect. Amygdalina).
1,2$Cap umber to sepia, almost to the margin
1,3$Cap clay-buff to orange-brown with a paler margin
2,0$Spores ellipsoid to ovoid : only found with dwarfish Salix species in arctic and boreal habitats : exsiccata coriaceous.$H. polare, p. 48
2,0$Spores ovoid to amygdaloid : with Salix in arc tic and boreal habitats as weil as in lowland bogs and sand dunes : exsiccata rather fragile.$H. nigellum, p. 52
3,0$Spores amygdaloid to subcitriform, often papillate, rarely > 12 µm long, strongly dextrinoid, D(3-)4 : exsiccata nearly always discoloured to dark brown : under Betula, Corylus, Quercus and possibly Fagus.$H. sordescens, p. 58
3,4$Spores amygdaloid to ovoid, non-papillate, often > 12 µm long, dextrinoid but never strongly so, D( 1-)2-3 : exsiccata typically not discoloured to dark brown : under Salix, possibly also other hosts
4,0$Stem 1-3 mm thick : cap 8-24 mm broad : in alpine habitats.$H. kuehneri, p. 52
4,5$Stem (2-)3-8 mm thick : cap 15-50 mm broad : in lowlands as weil as in alpine habitats
5,0$Cap margin white, contrasting the orange-brown centre : stem white with an orange-brown base : with Salix in humid Forest localities.$H. candidipes, p. 56
5,0$Stem and cap less contrasting, appearance mesophaeum-like : with Salix in arctic/ alpine regions, less often with Salix or Betula in boreal woodland.$H. monticola, p. 54

cs3,0$
rem$KEY C: Cortina absent : fruitbodies not rooting : smell radish-like : cystidia clavate to capitate : cap dominantly pale, not hygrophanous : spores strongly dextrinoid (D3-4), verruculose (01-2), with a mostly non-loosening perispore (PO-l) (sect, Velutipes, subsect. Velutipes).
1,0$Cheilocystidia on average < 6.0 µm broad at apex : only in moss in coniferous forests.$H. incarnatulum, p. 66
1,2$Cheilocystidia on average> 6.9 µm broad at apex : under various trees and shrubs
2,0$Cap diameter not exceeding 30 mm : spores on average 6.7-7.1 um broad : cheilocystidia distinctly clavate, apex on average 8.5-10.1 um broad : only in alpine habitats.$See H. alpinum, p. 78
2,0$Cap diameter mostly exceeding 30 mm : spores on average 6.0-6.6 µm broad : cheilocystidia distinctly clavate, apex on average 6.9-8.9 µm broad : in lowlands as well as in alpine habitats.$H. velutipes, p. 64

cs4,0$
rem$KEY D: Cortina absent : fruitbodies not rooting : sme1l radish-like : cystidia clavate to capitate, often in addition widened in the basal part : spore deposit umber to sepia (sect, Denudata, subsect. Denudata).
1,2$Species growing in alpine habitats with dwarf Salix or Dryas
1,5$Not growing in alpine habitats with dwarf Salix or Dryas
2,3$Stem 4-8 mm broad
2,4$Stem 1.5-3.5 mm broad
3,0$Cap < 30 mm broad : spores on average 6.7-7.1 µm broad.$H. alpinum, p. 78
3,0$Cap mostly > 30 mm broad : spores on average 5.5-6.5 µm broad.$H. crustuliniforme, p. 72
