CS11,0$
1,0$Section Hebeloma. Type: H. fastibile (Pers.: Fr.) P. Kumm. (= H. mesophaeum (Pers.) Quél) (Syn.: sect. Indusiata (Fr.) Sacc., type: H. mesophaeum (Pers.) Quél.) ///Cortina present; universal veil woolly, but sometimes very fugacious; stem never rooting; smell radish-like; spores ellipsoid to amygdaliform, dextrinoid or indextrinoid, perispore not loosening; cheilocystidia ventricose.
1,0$Subsection Hebeloma ///Spores ellipsoid, indextrinoid or weakly dextrinoid./// H. bruchetii, H. collariatum, H. marginatulum, H. mesophaeum, H. psammophilum, H. sordidum.
1,0$Subsection Amygdalina Vesterh. Type: H. sordescens Vesterh./// Spores +/- amygdaliform, +/- dextrinoid.///H.candidipes, H. kuehneri, H. monticola, H. nigellum, H. polare, H. sordescens. 

Cs12,0$
1,0$Section Sinapizantia (Quad.) Vesterh. Type: H. sinapizans (Paul.) Gill./// Cortina absent; universal veil typically only visible in primordia; cap +/- unicoloured brown; stem not rooting; smell radish-like; spores amygdaliform to citriform, dextrinoid, perispore loosening or not; cheilocystidia -.ventricose./// H. sinapizans.

Cs13,0$
1,0$Section Theobromina Beker, Eberhardt & Vesterh., sect. nov. Pileus brunneus. /// Cortina nulla. Stipes non radicans. Odor raphanoideus. Sporae amygdaloideae, valde dextrinoideae, perispora cohaerenti. Cheilocystidia cylindrica vel subclavata. Typus: Hebeloma theobrominum Quél./// Cap +/- unicoloured brown, often greyish pruinose; cortina absent; universal veil not observed; stem not rooting; smell +radish-like; spores amygdaliform, dextrinoid, perispore not loosening; cheilocystidia cylindrical to subclavate./// H. theobrominum

Cs14,0$

1,0$Section Velutipes Vesterh. Type: Hebeloma velutipes Bruchet./// Cortina absent; universal veil only visible in primordia; cap not hygrophanous; stem velutinate, never rooting; smell radish-like; spores amygdaliform to citriform, strongly dextrinoid; cheilocystidia narrowly to broadly clavate.
1,0$Subsection Velutipes///Spores amygdaloid, with a rather low ornamentation, without loosening perispore; cheilocystidia narrowly to broadly clavate.///H. incarnatulum, H. velutipes. 1,0$Subsection Aestivale Beker, Eberhardt & Vesterh., subsect. nov. ///Sporae citriformes, valde ornatae, valde dextrinoideae. Stipes non radicans. Cortina nulla. Odor raphanoideus. Typus: Hebeloma aestiuale Vesterh. ///Spores citriform, rather strongly ornamented, dextrinoid, with or without loosening perispore; cheilocystidia cylindrical to clavate or a mixture of ventricose and clavate. ///H. aestivale, H. quercetorum.


cs15,0$
1,0$Section Denudata Type: Hebeloma crustuliniforme (Bull.) Quél.///Cortina absent; universal veil typically only visible in primordia; stem never rooting; smell radish-like; spores amygdaliform to citriform, dextrinoid or indextrinoid, perispore loosening or not; cheilocystidia narrowly to broadly clavate or capitate, sometimes in addition widened in the lower half.
1,0$Subsection Denudata///Spore deposit umber to sepia or dark brick.///H. alpinum, H. alvarense, H. crustuliniforme, H. fragilipes, H. hiemale, H. helodes, H. lutense, H. minus, H. populinum, H. pusillum, H. subconcolor, H. vaccinum, H. vejlense.
1,0$Subsection Hebelomina (Maire) Beker, Eberhardt & Vesterh., stat. nov. Basionym genus Hebelomina Maire, Bull. Soc. Hist. Nat. Afrique du Nord 26: 13, 1935. Type: Hebelomina domardiana Maire. Spore deposit whitish. ///H. domardianum

Cs16,0$
1,0$Section Myxocybe (Fayod) Konr. & Maubl. Type: Hebeloma radicosum (Pers.) Quél. (Syn.: sect. Birri Groger, type Hebeloma birrus (Fr.) Sacc.).///Cortina absent; universal veil conspicuous or only visible in primordia; stem often +/- rooting; smell never radish-like; spore deposit umber; spores amygdaliform to inequilateral or cylindrical, dextrinoid, perispore not loosening in most species; cheilocystidia little differentiated, cylindrical to irregular, often short.
1,0$Subsection Myxocybe///Universal veil membranaceous, forming a ring on the stem; stem distinctly rooting; smell mostly like marzipan; cheilocystidia cylindrical to irregular, short.///H. radicosum.
1,0$Subsection Scabrispora Romagn. Type: Hebeloma cylindrosporum Romagn. (Syn.: Sect. Calyptrospora M. Bon. Type: H. calyptrosporum Bruchet).///Universal veil not known or only visible in the primordial state; stem in most species distinctly fusiform or rooting; smell variable, but never radish-like and never strong and sweetish; cheilocystidia cylindrical to irregular, mostly short.///H. birrus, H. circinans, H. cylindrosporum, H. laterinum, H. lundqvistii, H. syrjense. 
1,0$Subsection Sacchariolentia J.E. Lange ex M. Bon. Type: Hebeloma sacchariolens Quél. ///Universal veil not observed or only visible in the primordial state; stem distinctly or indistinctly fusiform or rooting; smell strong and sweetish; cheilocystidia variable in shape, often fairly large. ///H. fusipes, H. fusisporum, H. gigaspermum, H. hetieri, H. sacchariolens.


cs17,0$
1,0$Section Porphyrospova Konr. & Maubl. ex Vesterh., sect. nov. /// Sporae in cumulo rubrobrunneae. Lamellae roseae. Cortina nulla. Odor non raphanoideus. Sporae dextrinoideae. Cheilocystidia versiformes. Type: Hebeloma porphyrosporum (Paul.) Gill. (= H.sarcophyllum). Konrad & Maublanc (1948) proposed the section, but without a formal diagnosis. ///Cortina absent; universal veil not known; smell not radish-like; spore deposit brownish red; spores amygdaliform to citriform, dextrinoid, perispore not loosening; cheilocystidia variable in shape. ///H. sarcophyllum.

